const articleDescriptions={"The instruments of the orchestra": "Twenty-two instruments in their natural seating. Click any one for its range, its history, what it blends with, and the writing that defined it.", "The people you answer to.": "Temp tracks, spotting notes, deliverable specs. What each one is really asking for, and how to turn \"make it more epic\" into decisions.", "Reading a film-scoring brief": "Temp tracks, spotting notes, deliverable specs. What each one is really asking for, and how to turn \"make it more epic\" into decisions.", "The second temp track": "When a director falls for a composer's own rough sketch, the demo becomes a temp track the final score has to escape without ever replacing it outright.", "Decoding a revision note": "A note like \"make it feel more like that other cue\" is a compressed description of tempo, density or register, not a request to imitate the reference.", "Scoring to a cut that's still moving": "Writing to unlocked picture means anchoring structural hit points early while keeping bar counts and transitions flexible enough to survive re-cuts.", "Running a spotting session properly": "What to bring, who belongs in the room, and how to turn a spotting session into notes that still make sense three weeks later.", "Saying no to a note": "How to decline a director's note without turning it into a taste argument, and how to tell when trying the note beats debating it.", "A director who hears in references": "Translating \"make it feel like this, but not this\" into concrete decisions, and building a private shorthand with a recurring collaborator over time.", "Judgement at the desk.": "Why a line disappears, what doubling actually does, and how to write so the balance survives with nobody there to fix it.", "Balance without a conductor": "Why a line disappears, what doubling actually does, and how to write so the balance survives with nobody there to fix it.", "Fewer players, bigger sound": "A doubled string section isn't automatically bigger than a well-spaced quartet: register spread and attack alignment decide what actually reads as size.", "The horn's two faces": "The French horn has no fixed character: open or stopped technique, register and doubling decide whether the same line reads heroic or genuinely threatening.", "Voicing low brass without mud": "A correctly spelled trombone-and-tuba chord can still sound like mud at the bottom of the range: the fix is interval spacing, not fewer notes.", "What an ostinato is actually doing": "An ostinato isn't movie-music filler: it's rhythmic scaffolding, a tension device, and a way to raise stakes without raising volume.", "Writing the silence": "The bars with no notes are often the most deliberately engineered part of a cue: silence works as negative space, not leftover time.", "Percussion beyond the kit": "What a frame drum, a taiko, or a struck anvil actually signals to an audience, and how to build a percussion palette on purpose instead of by trend.", "The countermelody problem": "Why a countermelody that sounds fine solo can fight the actor's line at the mix, and how register and rhythm placement fix it before the dub stage.", "Contracts, rights, money.": "Ownership, publishing, and reuse rights explained without legalese: why the label on a scoring contract matters less than the clauses underneath it.", "What you actually own after delivery": "Ownership, publishing, and reuse rights explained without legalese: why the label on a scoring contract matters less than the clauses underneath it.", "The six clauses that matter more than the fee": "Exclusivity, credit, delivery and acceptance, ownership of stems, and rewrite scope: the language that shapes the job more than the fee does.", "How a cue actually gets paid, years later": "Why a cue sheet, not the invoice, decides whether a score keeps earning royalties, and how errors on it quietly cost composers money.", "What belongs in the quote besides your fee": "The budget lines composers forget when quoting only their own fee, and how leaving them out turns a profitable job into an unpaid one.", "Publishing splits, honestly": "Why signing away all your publishing isn't automatically a bad deal, and the contract terms worth negotiating instead of the split.", "Two different businesses, one job title": "Production library writing and commissioned film scoring share a job title but almost nothing else: how their economics and workflow diverge.", "What a music supervisor actually controls": "Where a supervisor's authority over licensing and song selection actually sits, versus where a composer needs to advocate directly.", "Negotiating the rewrite before it's requested": "How to build revision-round limits and paid-rewrite triggers into a deal before the first note is written, while the leverage still exists.", "Why a mockup convinces a director, not an orchestra": "What a MIDI mockup fakes convincingly, what it can't fake at all, and why that gap causes trouble once real players are reading from it.", "Choosing a library for the cue, not the demo": "Matching a sample library's real character to the cue at hand instead of the one that impressed you in a shootout video.", "Mockups that survive the session": "Building a mockup that translates cleanly into real parts, covering tempo map discipline and prep a copyist won't have to guess at.", "Where AI tools save time, and where they cost notes": "An honest, no-hype inventory of where AI tools genuinely save a working composer time, and where they quietly cost more to fix.", "A template that scales without starting over": "One bus structure and articulation map that works for a string quartet cue and a seventy-piece one alike.", "Humanizing is not velocity randomization": "Why a mockup still sounds mechanical after the humanize plugin, and what tying timing to actual phrase shape does instead.", "What the engineer needs before day one": "The concrete file prep (tempo maps, stems, reference mixes) that protects the first, most expensive hour of a session.", "What Is an Expression Map?": "Expression maps translate a named articulation into the keyswitch or controller a library needs. How they work in Cubase and in Dorico, and where the two differ.", "Building an Orchestral Template in Cubase": "What to decide before loading a single patch, the order that avoids rework, and how many days a first Cubase orchestral template really takes to build.", "Keyswitches or a Track per Articulation?": "Keyswitches, expression maps or one track for each articulation: what each costs in track count, readability and speed, and how to pick one for your writing.", "Build a Cubase Expression Map": "A practical walk-through for making a Cubase expression map: reading the keyswitch chart, creating slots and groups, adding dynamics, and testing the result.", "RAM and CPU for an Orchestral Template": "Why a fully purged template still fills your memory, how to measure what yours really uses, and where CPU limits tend to show up in a large orchestral session.", "Getting the second job from the first one": "What actually earns a callback after a first project wraps: reliability and communication, not the music alone.", "A demo reel that sounds like you": "Why imitating admired composers in a reel backfires, and what \"sounding like you\" actually means to someone hiring.", "What a music editor's opinion is worth": "Why a music editor's read on a composer travels into the mix room and the next hiring conversation.", "Ghostwriting and additional music, honestly": "The money, credit, and etiquette behind additional-music work, and where the unwritten boundaries usually sit.", "What a composer's agent actually does": "What representation actually changes at each career stage, and when seeking one is premature.", "Networking without a personality for it": "Concrete, low-charisma ways to build the relationships that actually generate work.", "Horror without the jump-scare sting": "Building sustained dread from harmony, timbre, and silence instead of the stock stinger.", "What a trailer cue is built to do": "Trailer scoring runs on its own structural rules: the build, the drop, the button.", "How adaptive game scoring actually works": "Game music has to survive an order the player controls, not the composer.", "Scoring comedy is a timing problem": "A comedic cue lives or dies on entrance point and rhythm, not melody.", "What a documentary score is actually for": "Supporting a true story's emotional weight without editorializing past what the facts show.", "Mickey-mousing, done well": "Tight sync between music and action isn't automatically dated, reclaiming a maligned technique.", "A main title theme that survives fifty listens": "What actually keeps a title theme from wearing out over a season or franchise.", "Scoring a two-hander without stepping on either actor": "Underscoring a two-person dialogue scene is really a frequency problem.", "Parts an orchestra won't silently resent": "The page turns, cue-ins, and clef choices that seventy sight-readers silently judge in the first minute.", "What a copyist actually fixes": "The invisible layer of correction between a raw score and a genuinely playable part.", "A click track that survives rubato": "Building a tempo map rigid where sync demands it and flexible everywhere else.", "Score formatting that signals experience": "The notational conventions that tell a reader this composer has done this before.", "Transposing instruments without a plugin": "Knowing transposition cold as a safety net for when the software gets it wrong.", "Dorico Playback Templates Explained": "How playback templates, endpoints and expression maps fit together in Dorico, and a clean way to build one so every new score plays back with your libraries.", "From sketch to delivery": "The real lifecycle of one cue, revision rounds and paperwork included, and an honest account of where the time actually goes.", "Running a session so the first hour isn't wasted": "Most of what makes a session run smoothly was decided the night before: paperwork, cue order, planned punch-ins.", "What \"make it warmer\" means at the mix": "Translating warmer, bigger, and cleaner into real EQ, balance and reverb decisions at the mix stage.", "The stem that gets used, and the one that doesn't": "A stem spec often asks for more splits than a dub stage will ever open: what to actually spend time on.", "Mixing Libraries From Different Developers": "Why orchestral libraries from different makers refuse to sit together, and the steps that fix it: one shared room, matched levels, depth, timing and tuning.", "BBC Symphony Orchestra: Core or Pro?": "A critical look at Spitfire's BBC Symphony Orchestra: what Core and Pro actually add, where brass, legato and RAM let you down, and who should buy it.", "Cinematic Studio Strings review": "CSS is famous for its legato. A critical look at what it does beautifully, what the latency costs you, what it cannot do, and whether it still earns a place in a template.", "Spitfire Symphony Orchestra, reviewed": "What Spitfire Symphony Orchestra really offers after the 2024 relaunch: Performance legato, a warm Lyndhurst sound, patchy woodwinds, and who it suits.", "Hollywood Strings 2 review": "Hollywood Strings 2 trades the first library's grand hall for close, dry detail. A critical look at the sound, the legato, the mic mixing, the price and who should buy it.", "Abbey Road One: an honest review": "Studio One's cinematic room, a 90-player orchestra and no legato in the core library. What Abbey Road One really offers, and who should buy it.", "Metropolis Ark 1 review": "Orchestral Tools' loud, dark Berlin orchestra, reviewed critically: what it does brilliantly, what it cannot do at all, and who should still buy it.", "Albion One: Spitfire's sketch orchestra": "Albion One gives you a 109-player orchestra that sounds big at once. A critical look at its strengths, its muddy mids, its limits, and who should buy it.", "Noire piano review": "Nils Frahm's Yamaha CFX as a Kontakt instrument, reviewed critically: the felt version is superb for intimate scoring, but the bell artefacts and missing pedal samples matter.", "Spitfire Tutti: one-finger orchestra": "Pre-orchestrated ensemble patches from SSO for \u00a399. A candid look at where Spitfire Tutti speeds up sketching, where its fixed voicings become a trap, and who should buy it.", "How to Write a Suno Prompt That Works": "A composer's method for the style field, structure tags and lyrics in Suno, with example prompts you can adapt, and a way to change one thing at a time.", "How to Describe a Cue to an AI Music Tool": "A cue is a job, not a mood. Write what the music must do, its shape, its palette and its limits, and turn a director's spotting words into a usable prompt.", "AI Music: Copyright and Commercial Use": "What Suno's terms allow, what the US Copyright Office has said about AI-generated work, how PROs treat it, and what to put in a client contract. Not legal advice.", "Suno Orchestral Prompts for Instrumental Cues": "Five worked prompts for orchestral and cinematic instrumentals in Suno, plus how to name the ensemble, the shape and the limits, and how to use the result as a sketch."};
function academy(){return `<main id="main" class="academy-page"><section class="academy-hero shell center">${label('ACADEMY')}<h1>The craft around<br>the notes.</h1><p>Most of what separates a working composer from a capable one is not harmony. It is knowing what a brief is really asking, why a line vanished in the mix, what a cor anglais will actually do to a phrase, how a contract clause changes a career, and where the hours go between sketch and delivery. Seventy-four pieces, free to read, no account, written for people already doing the job.</p></section><nav class="academy-nav" aria-label="Article categories">${academyGroups.map(([name],i)=>`<a href="#category-${i}">${name}</a>`).join('')}</nav><section id="category-0" class="academy-intro shell">${label('REFERENCE')}<h2>Seventy-four ways in.</h2><p>Organised the way the work itself is: the orchestra, the people you answer to, the craft, the business, the tools, the career, the genres, the paperwork, the studio floor.</p><div class="search-box"><input type="search" id="article-search" aria-label="Search academy" placeholder="Find a subject…"><span>⌕</span></div><a href="#" class="academy-feature article-card" data-article="The instruments of the orchestra">${label('REFERENCE')}<h3>The<br>instruments of<br>the orchestra</h3><p>Twenty-two instruments in their natural seating. Click any one for its range, its history, what it blends with, and the writing that defined it.</p><span>OPEN THE SEATING PLAN ↗</span></a></section>${academyGroups.slice(1).map(([tag,title,articles],n)=>`<section class="academy-category shell ${n>2?'blank-reveal':''}" id="category-${n+1}"><div class="${n>2?'reveal-content':''}">${label(tag)}<h2>${title}</h2><div class="article-grid">${articles.map((a,i)=>`<a href="#" class="article-card" data-article="${a}" style="--card-tint:${['#c66f49','#3b8797','#d4a83a','#8a6aa8'][n%4]}">${label(n===0&&i===0?'WORKING METHOD':tag)}<h3>${a}</h3><p>${articleDescriptions[a]||'Explore the practical craft around composing.'}</p><span>READ IT ↗</span></a>`).join('')}</div></div></section>`).join('')}<section class="page-cta blank-reveal shell"><div class="reveal-content center"><h2>Free tools sit alongside the reading.</h2><p>SAPIENZA's free tier answers orchestration questions on a clickable seating chart, the converter and viewer handle scores, and ANALISI reads your audio. None of them needs an account.</p><a class="button gold" href="/tools/">OPEN THE FREE TOOLS</a> <a class="button outline" href="/agents/">MEET THE SPECIALISTS</a></div></section></main>`}
